C = [
("h1", "5. The Voice in the Wilderness: Isaiah 40"),
("p", "Claim: the Gospels give John three descriptions drawn from Scripture, and they are different descriptions. One is Malachi’s messenger, one is Isaiah’s voice, and one is the spirit and power of Elias. Isaiah’s voice is the only one John applies to himself in the Gospels, and he applies it without the title of Elijah. The chapter reads Isaiah 40:1–5 in its own place and then reads how each Gospel brings it to John, so that the reader can see what is claimed for John by Isaiah and what is not."),
("sub", "5.1 Isaiah’s comfort"),
("p", "Isaiah 40 opens with a command to comfort: “Comfort ye, comfort ye my people, saith your God. Speak ye comfortably to Jerusalem, and cry unto her, that her warfare is accomplished, that her iniquity is pardoned: for she hath received of the LORD’s hand double for all her sins” (Isaiah 40:1, 2). Then the voice: “The voice of him that crieth in the wilderness, Prepare ye the way of the LORD, make straight in the desert a highway for our God” (Isaiah 40:3). The preparation is of a highway, and the highway is for “our God.” The valleys are lifted, the mountains made low, the crooked made straight, and “the glory of the LORD shall be revealed, and all flesh shall see it together: for the mouth of the LORD hath spoken it” (Isaiah 40:4, 5)."),
("p", "The speaker of the passage is not named. It is “the voice of him that crieth,” and the next verses show other voices: “The voice said, Cry. And he said, What shall I cry?” (Isaiah 40:6). The prophet gives no name to the one who cries in the wilderness, and he does not say that he is Elijah or that he is anyone but a voice. The passage is addressed to Jerusalem, its comfort is Jerusalem’s comfort, and its end is the glory of the LORD revealed."),
("sub", "5.2 How the Gospels bring it to John"),
("p", "All four Gospels apply Isaiah 40:3 to John. Mark joins it to Malachi: “As it is written in the prophets, Behold, I send my messenger before thy face … The voice of one crying in the wilderness, Prepare ye the way of the Lord, make his paths straight” (Mark 1:2, 3). The King James text prints “in the prophets,” and the quotation that follows is from two of them, Malachi and Isaiah. Matthew writes, “For this is he that was spoken of by the prophet Esaias, saying, The voice of one crying in the wilderness, Prepare ye the way of the Lord, make his paths straight” (Matthew 3:3). Luke carries on further into Isaiah: “Every valley shall be filled, and every mountain and hill shall be brought low; and the crooked shall be made straight, and the rough ways shall be made smooth; and all flesh shall see the salvation of God” (Luke 3:5, 6). Luke’s setting is dated by rulers: the fifteenth year of Tiberius Caesar, Pontius Pilate governor, “Annas and Caiaphas being the high priests,” and then, “the word of God came unto John the son of Zacharias in the wilderness” (Luke 3:1, 2)."),
("p", "John himself, in the fourth Gospel, uses the passage for himself when he is asked who he is: “I am the voice of one crying in the wilderness, Make straight the way of the Lord, as said the prophet Esaias” (John 1:23). This is the only place in the Gospels where John answers the question of who he is with a text of Scripture, and the text is Isaiah’s. He does not answer with Malachi 3:1 and he does not answer with Malachi 4:5."),
("sub", "5.3 What John preached and who came"),
("p", "The Gospels describe the message in the same terms. “In those days came John the Baptist, preaching in the wilderness of Judaea, and saying, Repent ye: for the kingdom of heaven is at hand” (Matthew 3:1, 2). Mark says, “John did baptize in the wilderness, and preach the baptism of repentance for the remission of sins” (Mark 1:4), and then, “there went out unto him all the land of Judaea, and they of Jerusalem, and were all baptized of him in the river of Jordan, confessing their sins” (Mark 1:5). Matthew says, “Then went out to him Jerusalem, and all Judaea, and all the region round about Jordan, and were baptized of him in Jordan, confessing their sins” (Matthew 3:5, 6)."),
("p", "The message and its audience are Israel’s. “All the land of Judaea, and they of Jerusalem” is the audience of Isaiah’s “comfort ye my people” and “speak ye comfortably to Jerusalem.” The call is to repent because the kingdom of heaven is at hand, and the kingdom is the one the prophets had announced to Israel. The volume reads the passages in this setting and does not carry the message of John over to anyone else. It is a message to Israel from a prophet of Israel about the kingdom, and its baptism is “the baptism of repentance.”"),
("sub", "5.4 Mark’s two words and John’s dress"),
("p", "Mark describes John: “John was clothed with camel’s hair, and with a girdle of a skin about his loins; and he did eat locusts and wild honey” (Mark 1:6). Matthew has “his raiment of camel’s hair, and a leathern girdle about his loins” (Matthew 3:4). Chapter 2 put beside this the description given to Ahaziah of the man who met his messengers, “an hairy man, and girt with a girdle of leather about his loins” (2 Kings 1:8). The likeness is in the girdle of leather and the rough dress. The Gospels do not say that John dressed so in order to be taken for Elijah, and they do not draw the comparison. The comparison is the reader’s, and the volume offers it only as a likeness of the text’s wording, one that fits the sentence of Luke 1:17 and settles nothing alone."),
("ctbl", "How the Gospels describe John from Scripture.", [
    ("Description", "Source", "Who applies it"),
    ("My messenger, to prepare the way", "Malachi 3:1", "The Lord (Matthew 11:10; Luke 7:27); Mark 1:2"),
    ("The voice of one crying in the wilderness", "Isaiah 40:3", "Matthew 3:3; Mark 1:3; Luke 3:4; John himself (John 1:23)"),
    ("In the spirit and power of Elias", "Malachi 4:5, 6 echoed", "Gabriel (Luke 1:17)"),
    ("Elias, if ye will receive it", "Malachi 4:5", "The Lord (Matthew 11:14)"),
    ("Not Elias", "Malachi 4:5", "John (John 1:21)"),
  ], [0.36, 0.24, 0.4]),
("sub", "5.5 What the volume takes from the passages"),
("bul", [
    "Isaiah 40:3 speaks of a voice crying in the wilderness and a highway prepared for “our God”; the voice is anonymous and the comfort is Jerusalem’s.",
    "All four Gospels apply Isaiah 40:3 to John, and John applies it to himself in John 1:23.",
    "John answers the question of who he is with Isaiah’s voice and not with a title of Elijah.",
    "John’s preaching and baptism are to Israel: “all the land of Judaea, and they of Jerusalem.”",
    "The likeness between John’s dress and Elijah’s is in the wording of the text and is noted without being pressed.",
  ]),
]
